on billions of pounds of damage  and of course harm to wildlife  well a little earlier i spoke to one of  the reports authors  professor frederica otto from the  grantham institute for climate change  and environment at imperial college  london i began by asking her how they  reached their conclusions  so what we did in this in this review  paper is that we  we started at the um at last year's ipcc  report and then looked at basically a  decade of  research on  all sorts of extreme weather events  across the world and the role of climate  change in these events and looked at  what are the global conclusions that we  can draw and one of the key conclusions  is that every heatwave that is happening  today has been made more likely and more  intense because of human-induced climate  change  and where are those figures coming from  tell us a bit more about the data and  what you extrapolated  so we haven't looked at  data at measured data but we have looked  at  um at a large  range of published  reports and published academic papers  that have all used  observational data from weather stations  all sorts of other different  observational data products and  many different climate models and all  these papers come to the same conclusion  uh when it is concerned heat waves not  only that climate change does play a  role and makes them more likely more  intense but actually a lot more likely  climate change is a real game changer  when it comes to extreme heat  and you found when it comes to drought  that it's less clear-cut why is that  basically the relationship between  climate change and drought is is much  more complex than than with heat because  um depending on where in the region you  are uh what makes a drought is is quite  different so in regions where for  example it's already uh semi-arid so for  example eastern africa when you have you  have we have an increase in temperature  but that actually doesn't play a big  role for making the droughts worse  because  it has already been always so that  whenever there was rain it evaporated  quickly so there would only be a change  in drought if climate change would make  the rain less and that is for example in  eastern africa not or only to a very  small degree the case but in other parts  of the world like southern africa or the  mediterranean that is the case and so  there climate change does  increase drought risk  and you also warn that the financial  cost and sadly the number of lives being  lost due to climate change are being  underestimated explain just how  dangerous that is and also what can be  done to rectify that  yeah so first of all the reason for that  is that  as i said climate change  is really really  a game changer in heat extremes but in  many parts of the world we actually do  not record heat extremes and so that  means we don't record the death  associated with this even though we know  from the parts of the world where we do  have data that heat waves are the most  deadliest extreme events and we also  don't really comprehensively record the  economic costs and that means that when  we are thinking about how to adapt to  climate change we might ignore the most  obvious and most most dangerous changes  if we don't record heat impacts and heat  extremes  dr frederica otto thank you so much for  coming on the show and explaining that  research  thank you  let's get some more of the day's climate  news now g7 leaders say they'll work to  establish a climate club by the end of  this year a communique issued after  today's summit says it will aim to  advance ambitious and transparent  climate mitigation policies and reduce  intense emissions from participating  countries france's president macron also  told the summit in germany that major  world relations must continue with  climate and environmental goals despite  current pressures on energy markets  the united nations secretary-general  antonio guterres says the world is  facing an ocean emergency as thousands  of activists scientists and leaders  gather at the un ocean conference in the  portuguese capital lisbon they're  calling for measures to strengthen  protection of the seas  sadly  we have taken the ocean for granted  and today we face what i would call an  ocean emergency  we must turn the tide  global heating  is pushing ocean temperatures to record  levels  creating fiercer and more frequent storm  emergency services and charities are  working to rescue people in turkey after  flooding left many stranded heavy rain  in the northwest of the country has  caused rivers to overflow and bridges to  collapse the extreme weather comes less  than a week after turkey battled  wildfires in the southwest of the  country near the popular holiday resort  of marmarus  the famous notre dame cathedral in paris  will be redesigned to help mitigate the  effects of climate change the tourist  attraction is being rebuilt following a  fire in 2019. new plans involve planting  over 30 percent more vegetation and  installing a new cooling system a 5  millimeter layer of water will stream in  front of the cathedral during heat waves  aimed at reducing temperatures by  several degrees  now wimbledon is back this week and for  the first time in three years the game  sets and matches being played in front  of capacity crowds that's great for the  sporting atmosphere though potentially  not so good for the environment but the  all england lawn tennis club which is a  signatory to the un sport for climate  action framework is aiming to make these  championships the most sustainable yet  our sports presenter jackie bell trout  spoke to the club's sustainability  manager hatty park about the changes  well there are a lot of things different  at wimbledon this year and  most noticeably that strawberries and  cream are now served in a cardboard box  they still cost 2 pound 50 which is  pretty good value but they come in a  beautiful sort of recycled cardboard box  and the person behind that decision is  hatty park who's a sustainability um  chief in charge  of sorting all those things out and and  hattie um  what was behind all the decisions that  you've made for this year well it was  all part of our overall aim to be  environment positive so looking at  actions we can take to address climate  change resource depletion support nature  and we also know  that during the championships we have an  amazing opportunity to really engage  with everyone that comes here so we want  people to be part of it as well so you  know the packaging that the strawberries  come in is is part of that and thinking  about other ways that we can involve  everyone whether they're watching at  home or coming to the championships in  being part of this environment positive  journey that's so important yeah well  it's much more obvious this year because  as well as that you know you used to get  pims in a sort of throwaway plastic cup  now you've got to buy your plastic cup  and if you want your money back you've  got to bring the cup back don't you  exactly well that's really important to  promote a culture of reuse rather than  single use and throw away so that's what  we're aiming to do  you know we hope guests have a brilliant  day out we would like them to return the  cups to us they're taken away washed and  then come back so they can be used again  and again year after year or you can put  them in a box into the charity box and  then that pound goes to charity which is  a great idea and there's also um a  beautiful i haven't seen it yet but it's  going to be a beautiful wall of flowers  or plants tell me about that there's a  wall you know we present tennis in an  english garden there's an awful lot of  gorgeous planting around on the side of  number one court there's a living wall  which uh is an amazing backdrop for  people watching tennis on the hill but  it was deliberately planted with  flowering plants to encourage  pollinators  and you also which i find fascinating  collected all the strings from the  racket because the players go through a  lot of rackets during wimbledon the  strings break collected all of the old  strings cut off the rackets and did what  well we yeah we collected them last year  we've sent them away they've been ground  up mixed with other waste plastic and  they've come back in the form of  countertops on some ticket resolution  kiosks so they're back in the grounds  being useful i think that's the main  thing is that you know we wanted to get  something useful from those strings and  we collect them again this year so are  you going to go around now and just make  sure that everybody is doing what they  should be doing with their plastic cups  and with their cardboard strawberry  boxes yes i am i'm the i'm think i'm the  bin police i you know i try to think  strategically and then during the  championships i'm looking in the bins  hattie park bin police thank you very  much have a wonderful championship yeah  lots of changes around here but you know  changes for the better  and just before we leave you we want to  know your climate questions anything  that's ever mystified you or whatever  you want to get to the bottom of it's  all for a brand new weekend climate show  hosted by the environmental broadcaster  and author tom heap it starts this  saturday the 2nd of july and if you scan  the qr code on your screen right now you  can send us the questions that you want  to see answered on the show  that is all from us for now thanks so  much for watching
 

